{
 "id": "2023-harrison-awe",
 "name": "Awe and Beauty in Nature Photos",
 "description": "Does feeling connected to nature make nature look more beautiful, and is attention the reason? Participants view 14 photographs of natural scenes (waterfalls, mountains, clouds, rivers, sea) for 10 s each, in the paper's fixed order after one practice photograph, and then answer seven items on a 1–7 scale: “Did you find this picture…” pleasing, beautiful, awe-inspiring, moving, profound, boring, plus “How closely were you paying attention to the picture?”. Aesthetic emotion = mean of awe-inspiring, moving, profound and boring (reversed). Uses the author's 15 stimuli from OSF (osf.io/wpxcn), selected from Pexels. Rebuilt from Harrison (2023), PeerJ 11: e14926 (82 participants; attention mediated the link between connectedness to nature and aesthetic pleasure and beauty). Differences from the original study: (1) the 14-item Connectedness to Nature Scale (used in the paper with the copyright holders' permission) is replaced by the openly published 6-item Nature Relatedness short form (NR-6, Nisbet & Zelenski 2013, 1–5); (2) the Openness/Intellect subscale of the Big Five Aspect Scales is left out; (3) online with mouse or touch buttons instead of a lab booth with E-Prime and keyboard responses.",
